The rotations of space, $SO(3)$, are a curved manifold of dimension three, and every way of
writing one down has a defect: a matrix has nine numbers and six constraints, three angles lose a
degree of freedom at some attitude, and a unit quaternion names each rotation twice. This note
takes the unit quaternions, which multiply and renormalize cheaply, and works out what the two
names cost. It gives the rotations their [retractions](../retraction/), the maps a computation
needs of them, and the ways in and out through angles, directions and matrices.

## Quaternions and rotations

A quaternion is $q = (w, u)$ with $w \in \R$ and $u \in \R^3$, a vector of $\R^4$. Here and below a
tuple of vectors is the column vector with its entries stacked. The product is

$$
	(w_1, u_1) \otimes (w_2, u_2) = \bigl(w_1 w_2 - u_1 \cdot u_2,\ w_1 u_2 + w_2 u_1 + u_1 \times u_2\bigr), @tag(product)
$$

the conjugate is $\bar q = (w, -u)$, and we write $\operatorname{vec}(q) = u$ and $w(q) = w$. The
unit quaternions $S^3 = \{q : \lVert q \rVert = 1\}$ are a group under $\otimes$, with identity
$1 = (1, 0)$ and inverse $\bar q$. Each is a rotation,

$$
	\pi(q) = (w^2 - \lVert u \rVert^2)\, I + 2\, u u\transpose + 2 w\, [u]_\times , @tag(rotation)
$$

where $[u]_\times$ is the skew matrix with $[u]_\times x = u \times x$. It is the rotation by the
angle $\theta$ about the unit axis $a$ when $q = (\cos \tfrac\theta2, \sin \tfrac\theta2\, a)$, and
it acts on a vector as $\pi(q)\, x = x + 2 w\, (u \times x) + 2\, u \times (u \times x)$. The map
$\pi : S^3 \to SO(3)$ is a homomorphism, onto, and two to one: $\pi(q) = \pi(-q)$, and no other
quaternion gives the same rotation.

So the rotations are the unit quaternions up to sign. The group of two elements $\{\pm 1\}$ acts on
$S^3$ by $q \mapsto -q$, freely, and $SO(3) = S^3 / \{\pm 1\}$ is its
[quotient](../quotient-manifold/), with $\pi$ the quotient map. Everything in that note applies: a
map written on quaternions is a map of rotations when it is unchanged by the sign of each
quaternion it reads, and a map into the rotations when the sign of its result is all that the signs
of its arguments change. The composition $q_1 \otimes q_2$, the inverse $\bar q$, the relative
rotation $\bar q_1 \otimes q_2$ that carries the frame of $q_1$ to that of $q_2$, and the action on
a vector are all of these kinds, since $(\pm q_1) \otimes (\pm q_2) = \pm (q_1 \otimes q_2)$.

Tangent coordinates of a rotation are, throughout, a rotation vector in the frame of the point: a
retraction of this note satisfies

$$
	\pi(q \oplus \tangent) = \pi(q) \bigl(I + [\tangent]_\times\bigr) + O(\lVert \tangent \rVert^2) , @tag(frame)
$$

a perturbation on the right, in the body's own axes. Since $q \otimes (w, u) \otimes \bar q = (w,
\pi(q)\, u)$ for a unit $q$, the same perturbation written on the left, in the outer frame, is
$\pi(q)\, \tangent$.

## No continuous choice of quaternion

One might hope to pick one quaternion for each rotation, once and for all, and so be rid of the
sign. It cannot be done continuously.

<Statement {...noSection} />

<Proof>

Suppose $s$ is one. Its image $s(SO(3))$ is compact, and so is $-s(SO(3))$, since $q \mapsto -q$ is
continuous. They are disjoint, since $s(R) = -s(R')$ gives $R = \pi(s(R)) = \pi(-s(R')) = R'$ and
then $s(R) = -s(R)$, which no unit quaternion is. And they cover $S^3$, since a $q$ with
$\pi(q) = R$ is $s(R)$ or $-s(R)$. So $S^3$ is the union of two disjoint nonempty closed sets, which
contradicts its connectedness.

</Proof>

Every formula that reads a quaternion off a rotation is therefore discontinuous somewhere. The
usual reading of a quaternion off a rotation matrix $X$ is a choice between four,

$$
	\begin{aligned}
	c_0(X) &= (1 + X_{11} + X_{22} + X_{33},\ X_{32} - X_{23},\ X_{13} - X_{31},\ X_{21} - X_{12}), \\
	c_1(X) &= (X_{32} - X_{23},\ 1 + X_{11} - X_{22} - X_{33},\ X_{12} + X_{21},\ X_{13} + X_{31}), \\
	c_2(X) &= (X_{13} - X_{31},\ X_{12} + X_{21},\ 1 - X_{11} + X_{22} - X_{33},\ X_{23} + X_{32}), \\
	c_3(X) &= (X_{21} - X_{12},\ X_{13} + X_{31},\ X_{23} + X_{32},\ 1 - X_{11} - X_{22} + X_{33}),
	\end{aligned}
	\qquad
	q = \frac{c_m(X)}{\lVert c_m(X) \rVert}, @tag(from-matrix)
$$

with $m$ the index of the largest of the four entries $c_m(X)_m$. At $X = \pi(q)$, with $q = (q_0,
q_1, q_2, q_3)$, [](eq:rotation) gives $c_m(X) = 4 q_m\, q$, so the result is $\operatorname{sign}(q_m)\,
q$: the quaternion over $X$ whose $m$-th coordinate is positive. Since $c_m(X)_m = 4 q_m^2$ and the
largest of the $q_m^2$ is at least $\tfrac14$, the chosen vector has norm at least $2$ and the
division is safe. Within each choice of $m$ the map is smooth, and where the choice changes the
result may change sign, as [%full](statement:no-section) says it must somewhere.

## The exponential and the logarithm

The exponential takes a rotation vector to the rotation about it by its length:

$$
	\Exp(\omega) = \Bigl(\cos \tfrac\theta2,\ \frac{\sin(\theta/2)}{\theta}\, \omega\Bigr),
	\qquad \theta = \lVert \omega \rVert . @tag(exp)
$$

It is a diffeomorphism of the open ball of radius $2\pi$ onto $S^3 \setminus \{-1\}$. It is how a
rate becomes a rotation: an attitude turning at the constant body rate $\omega$ for a time $\Delta
t$ moves from $q_k$ to $q_{k+1} = q_k \otimes \Exp(\omega\, \Delta t)$, exactly. Its inverse on
$S^3 \setminus \{-1\}$ is the logarithm,

$$
	\Log(q) = \frac{2 \atantwo(n, w)}{n}\, u, \qquad n = \lVert u \rVert, @tag(log)
$$

a rotation vector of length $2 \atantwo(n, w) \in [0, 2\pi)$. The logarithm is a map of the unit
quaternions and not of the rotations: for a rotation by $\theta$ about $a$ it gives $\theta a$ at
$q$ and $(2\pi - \theta)(-a)$ at $-q$, two names of one rotation. The rotation's own logarithm is
the rotation vector of the shorter turn,

$$
	\rv(q) = \Log\bigl(s(q)\, q\bigr), \qquad s(q) = \begin{cases} 1 & w \ge 0, \\ -1 & w < 0, \end{cases} @tag(rotation-vector)
$$

with $\rv(-q) = \rv(q)$ and length in $[0, \pi]$. It is discontinuous at the half-turns, $w = 0$,
where it jumps from $\pi a$ to $-\pi a$: two rotation vectors of one rotation, equally short.

The left and right Jacobians of the exponential are the matrices with $\Exp(\omega + \varepsilon) =
\Exp(J_l(\omega)\, \varepsilon) \otimes \Exp(\omega) = \Exp(\omega) \otimes \Exp(J_r(\omega)\,
\varepsilon)$ to first order in $\varepsilon$. They are

$$
	J_l(\omega) = I + A\, [\omega]_\times + B\, [\omega]_\times^2, \quad
	J_r(\omega) = J_l(-\omega) = I - A\, [\omega]_\times + B\, [\omega]_\times^2, \quad
	A = \frac{1 - \cos \theta}{\theta^2}, \quad B = \frac{\theta - \sin \theta}{\theta^3}, @tag(jacobians)
$$

and their inverses, for $\theta < 2\pi$,

$$
	J_l(\omega)^{-1} = I - \tfrac12 [\omega]_\times + C\, [\omega]_\times^2, \quad
	J_r(\omega)^{-1} = I + \tfrac12 [\omega]_\times + C\, [\omega]_\times^2, \quad
	C = \frac{1}{\theta^2}\Bigl(1 - \frac\theta2 \cot \frac\theta2\Bigr). @tag(jacobian-inverses)
$$

Each is a polynomial in $[\omega]_\times$, so the inverses are checked by multiplying out with
$[\omega]_\times^3 = -\theta^2 [\omega]_\times$, and $J_r(\omega) = J_l(\omega)\transpose$, since
$[\omega]_\times$ is skew. The coefficients $A$, $B$ and $C$, like $\sin(\theta/2)/\theta$ in
[](eq:exp) and $2\atantwo(n, w)/n$ in [](eq:log), are smooth functions whose closed forms divide by
a quantity that vanishes at the identity. How such a coefficient is computed is the subject of
[a later note](../removable-singularities/).

The exponential gives a retraction, $q \oplus \tangent = q \otimes \Exp(\tangent)$, the
*exponential chart*. It is the chart most often written down, and it is not global: it is
invertible only on the ball of radius $2\pi$, and as a chart of the rotations only on the ball of
radius $\pi$, with the half-turns on its boundary. A random tangent vector with unbounded support
pushed through it lands outside the chart with positive probability. The next two sections give
the two charts that are defined, and invertible, on all of $\R^3$.

## The stereographic chart

The first is the stereographic projection of the sphere $S^3$ from the point opposite the centre:

$$
	q \oplus \tangent = q \otimes \frac{\bigl(1 - s,\ \tangent / 2\bigr)}{1 + s}, \quad s = \tfrac1{16} \lVert \tangent \rVert^2;
	\qquad
	p \ominus q = \frac{4\, \operatorname{vec}(\bar q \otimes p)}{1 + w(\bar q \otimes p)} . @tag(stereographic)
$$

<Statement {...stereographic} />

<Proof>

Write $\delta = \delta(\tangent)$. Its norm is $((1 - s)^2 + 4s)^{1/2} / (1 + s) = 1$, so
$q \otimes \delta$ is a unit quaternion. With $w(\delta) = (1 - s)/(1 + s)$ and
$\lVert \operatorname{vec}(\delta) \rVert = (\lVert \tangent \rVert / 2)/(1 + s)$, $\delta$ is the
rotation by $\theta$ about $\tangent$ with $\cos \tfrac\theta2 = (1-s)/(1+s)$ and $\sin
\tfrac\theta2 = (\lVert \tangent \rVert / 2)/(1+s)$, so $\tan \tfrac\theta4 = \sin \tfrac\theta2 /
(1 + \cos \tfrac\theta2) = \lVert \tangent \rVert / 4$. As $\lVert \tangent \rVert$ runs over
$[0, \infty)$, $\theta$ runs over $[0, 2\pi)$, so $\delta$ runs over every unit quaternion but $-1$,
once each; $\delta$ is the inverse of the stereographic projection of $S^3$ from $-1$ onto the
hyperplane $w = 0$, at $\tangent / 4$. Left multiplication by $q$ carries $S^3 \setminus \{-1\}$ to
$S^3 \setminus \{-q\}$. The inverse follows from $\delta = \bar q \otimes p$ and $\operatorname{vec}(
\delta) / (1 + w(\delta)) = \tangent / 4$, which $s$ cancels from. Last, $\delta(\tangent) = (1,
\tangent / 2) + O(\lVert \tangent \rVert^2)$, and [](eq:rotation) at it is $I + [\tangent]_\times +
O(\lVert \tangent \rVert^2)$; $\pi$ is a homomorphism.

</Proof>

The chart's coordinate of a rotation by $\theta$ has the length $4 \tan(\theta / 4) = \theta +
\theta^3 / 48 + O(\theta^5)$. These are the *modified Rodrigues parameters* of attitude estimation,
scaled by $4$ so that they agree with the rotation vector to first order. The chart is the left
translation of the chart at the identity, $q \oplus \tangent = q \otimes (1 \oplus \tangent)$, and
since $\delta$ depends on $\tangent$ only through its direction and its length, which $\pi(q)$
preserves,

$$
	q \oplus \tangent = (1 \oplus \pi(q)\, \tangent) \otimes q @tag(conjugation)
$$

exactly, not only to first order: the chart written with its perturbation on the left is the same
chart, its coordinates rotated by $\pi(q)$.

It is a chart of $S^3$, and so it must tell $q$ from $-q$, which are different points of $S^3$.
Where it puts the two is the whole of what the sign costs.

<Statement {...antipode} />

<Proof>

Write $\bar\mu \otimes q = (w, u)$, so that $\tangent = 4u / (1 + w)$ and, since $\lVert u
\rVert^2 = 1 - w^2$,

$$
	\lVert \tangent \rVert^2 = \frac{16 \lVert u \rVert^2}{(1 + w)^2} = \frac{16 (1 - w)}{1 + w} ,
$$

which is below $16$ exactly when $w = \langle q, \mu \rangle > 0$. Both $\bar\mu \otimes (-q)$ and
$\overline{(-\mu)} \otimes q$ are $(-w, -u)$, whose coordinate is $-4u / (1 - w)$, and

$$
	-\frac{16}{\lVert \tangent \rVert^2}\, \tangent = -\frac{(1 + w)}{(1 - w)} \cdot \frac{4u}{1 + w} = -\frac{4u}{1 - w} .
$$

For the lengths, the point on the side of $\mu$ is at the angle $\theta \in [0, \pi]$ from $\mu$ by
[%full](statement:stereographic), which is the angle between the rotations, and the other at $2\pi -
\theta$, whose coordinate has the length $4 \tan(\pi/2 - \theta/4) = 4 \cot(\theta/4)$.

</Proof>

So the map from one sign to the other is the inversion in the sphere of radius $4$, followed by
negation. The ball of radius $4$ is the chart of the near sides, one point for each rotation within
less than a half-turn of $\pi(\mu)$, and its complement holds the far sides. A rotation close to
$\pi(\mu)$, given by the quaternion on the far side, has a coordinate of length $4 \cot(\theta/4)$,
which grows without bound as the rotations approach each other: read in this chart, a rotation
given with the other sign is as far from $\mu$ as a point can be, exactly when it is nearest.

The sign is not only a matter of single points. A turn of $2\pi$ about an axis is the identity
rotation and the quaternion $-1$: rotations about one axis by $\alpha_1, \dots, \alpha_n$ with
$\sum_k \alpha_k = 2\pi$ compose to the identity, while their quaternions, each taken with $w > 0$,
multiply to $-1$. Angles add modulo $4\pi$ on $S^3$ and modulo $2\pi$ on the rotations. A chain of
relative rotations that closes on itself as rotations may therefore fail to close as quaternions,
and quaternions fitted to it link by link must take up the missing turn somewhere along the chain.

## The Cayley chart

A chart of the rotations must not tell $q$ from $-q$. The second global chart is the familiar
first-order update of a quaternion, normalized.

<Statement {...cayley} />

<Proof>

The norm of $(1, \tangent / 2)$ is $\sqrt{1 + \lVert \tangent \rVert^2 / 4}$, so $\gamma(\tangent)
\in S^3$, with $w(\gamma) > 0$ and $\lVert \operatorname{vec}(\gamma) \rVert / w(\gamma) =
\lVert \tangent \rVert / 2$; it is the rotation by $\theta$ about $\tangent$ with $\tan(\theta / 2) =
\lVert \tangent \rVert / 2$, so $\theta \in [0, \pi)$, every such rotation once. Negating $q$
negates $q \otimes \gamma$. Negating $p$ or $q$ negates $\bar q \otimes p$, whose $\operatorname{vec}$
and $w$ change sign together, so their ratio does not. For $p = q \otimes \gamma(\tangent)$, $\bar q
\otimes p = \gamma(\tangent)$ and $2 \operatorname{vec}(\gamma) / w(\gamma) = \tangent$; and every
rotation by less than $\pi$ from $\pi(q)$ is $\pi(q \otimes \delta)$ for a $\delta$ with
$w(\delta) > 0$, which is $\gamma$ of $2 \operatorname{vec}(\delta) / w(\delta)$. The first-order
form and the last identity follow as for the stereographic chart: $\gamma(\tangent) = (1, \tangent /
2) + O(\lVert \tangent \rVert^2)$, and $\gamma$ depends on $\tangent$ only through its direction
and its length.

</Proof>

The retraction commutes with the sign and its inverse is unchanged by the sign of either argument,
which are the conditions of [%full](statement:math/quotient-manifold/descent). So this is a
retraction on $SO(3)$ itself, with its inverse, computed through whichever quaternions the two
rotations happen to be given by. On matrices it is the Cayley transform, $\pi(q \oplus \tangent) =
\pi(q)\, (I - \tfrac12 [\tangent]_\times)^{-1} (I + \tfrac12 [\tangent]_\times)$. Its coordinate of a
rotation by $\theta$ has the length

$$
	2 \tan(\theta / 2) = \theta + \theta^3 / 12 + O(\theta^5) ,
$$

a little farther from the angle than the stereographic chart's. A rotation has one coordinate at
$\mu$, of length $2\tan(\theta/2)$ for $\theta \in [0, \pi)$ its angle from $\pi(\mu)$, whatever
the signs. Nothing in the chart reads which quaternion a rotation was given by, so the missing turn
of the last section cannot arise: a chain of relative rotations that closes as rotations is fitted
with nothing left over.

What the chart misses, at each $\mu$, is the rotations a half-turn from $\pi(\mu)$: not one point,
as the stereographic chart misses $-\mu$, but a surface, the rotations by $\pi$ about every axis,
composed with $\mu$. There $w(\bar\mu \otimes p) = 0$ and the inverse is undefined, and near it the
coordinate is large. The two charts thus put their cuts in different places. The stereographic
chart is finite at every half-turn, with a coordinate of length $4$, and is cut at the antipode,
the point a mistaken sign lands on. The Cayley chart is cut at the half-turns, which a pair of
rotations reaches only by differing as much as two rotations can.

## Agreement with the exponential

Three retractions are now in hand, and a derivative read in tangent charts does not depend on
which is used.

<Statement {...agreement} />

<Proof>

From $(1 - s)/(1 + s) = 1 - 2s + O(s^2)$ with $s = \lVert \tangent \rVert^2 / 16$, the
stereographic chart has $1 \oplus \tangent = \bigl(1 - \tfrac18 \lVert \tangent \rVert^2,\ \tfrac12
\tangent\bigr) + O(\lVert \tangent \rVert^3)$. From $(1 + \lVert \tangent \rVert^2 / 4)^{-1/2} = 1 -
\tfrac18 \lVert \tangent \rVert^2 + O(\lVert \tangent \rVert^4)$, the Cayley chart has the same
expansion, and so has $\Exp(\tangent)$, from $\cos \tfrac\theta2 = 1 - \tfrac18 \theta^2 +
O(\theta^4)$ and $\sin(\theta/2)/\theta = \tfrac12 + O(\theta^2)$. All three charts are left
translations of their charts at the identity, so they agree at every point to second order, and so
do their inverses. The [derivative in charts](../derivatives-in-charts/) of a map $\map$ at
$\point$ is the Jacobian at $0$ of $\tangent \mapsto R_\otherPoint^{-1}(\map(R_\point(\tangent)))$,
and the local representatives of $\map$ in two charts that agree to second order agree to second
order at $0$, with their first and second derivatives there. Last, $\Exp(\omega + \varepsilon)
\ominus \Exp(\omega)$ is the chart coordinate at $1$ of $\Exp(\omega)^{-1} \otimes \Exp(\omega +
\varepsilon) = \Exp(J_r(\omega)\, \varepsilon) + O(\lVert \varepsilon \rVert^2)$, which is
$J_r(\omega)\, \varepsilon + O(\lVert \varepsilon \rVert^2)$; and $\Log$ is the inverse of $\Exp$.

</Proof>

So the Jacobians of the textbooks, derived in exponential coordinates, are the derivatives in
either global chart, and a formula that carries an uncertainty through a rate by hand writes $J_r$
into it. The charts differ at third order, in how far a finite step goes, and in where they are
cut.

## Interpolation and distance

The geodesic from $q_1$ to $q_2$, at the parameter $t \in \R$, is

$$
	q_1 \otimes \Exp\bigl(t \Log(\bar q_1 \otimes q_2)\bigr), @tag(slerp)
$$

which runs at a constant rate along the great circle of $S^3$ through $q_1$ and $q_2$, reaching
$q_2$ at $t = 1$ by the arc that avoids $-q_1$. With $\rv$ in place of $\Log$ it is the rotations'
interpolation, along the shorter of the two turns from one attitude to the other, and
discontinuous where the relative rotation is a half-turn and the two turns are equally short. The
two agree whenever $\langle q_1, q_2 \rangle \ge 0$.

The angle between two rotations, the length of the shorter turn from one to the other, is

$$
	\angle(q_1, q_2) = \lVert \rv(\bar q_1 \otimes q_2) \rVert = 2 \atantwo\bigl(\lVert
	\operatorname{vec}(r) \rVert,\ \lvert w(r) \rvert\bigr) \in [0, \pi], \qquad r = \bar q_1 \otimes
	q_2 . @tag(angle)
$$

It is the same at $\pm q_1$ and $\pm q_2$, and smooth where $0 < \angle < \pi$: at $0$ it is a norm,
with no derivative, and at $\pi$ a maximum, where $\lvert w(r) \rvert$ turns. Its square is smooth
at $0$, and is what a cost on an attitude's error is written with.

## Angles and directions

Rotations are given and reported as an axis and an angle, as a pair of directions, and as Euler
angles. Each is a way in and out, and none is a chart.

**Axis and angle.** The rotation by $\theta$ about an axis $a \ne 0$ is $(\cos \tfrac\theta2,\ \sin
\tfrac\theta2\, a / \lVert a \rVert)$, smooth in both. The way back is the rotation vector split
into its length and its direction, and the direction is undefined at the identity. A computation
that differentiates through an axis and an angle is better served by the rotation vector, which
has no such point.

**From two directions.** The rotation of least angle carrying the direction of $a$ to that of $b$ is
the normalization of

$$
	\bigl(\lVert a \rVert \lVert b \rVert + a \cdot b,\ a \times b\bigr), @tag(two-vectors)
$$

since for unit vectors at the angle $\theta$ about the axis $n$ this is $(1 + \cos \theta,
\sin \theta\, n) = 2 \cos \tfrac\theta2 \bigl(\cos \tfrac\theta2, \sin \tfrac\theta2\, n\bigr)$.
It is smooth for $a, b \ne 0$ except where $b$ is opposite to $a$: there the vector vanishes, every
half-turn about an axis perpendicular to $a$ is a rotation of least angle, and the map is undefined.

**Euler angles.** A sequence of axes $(i, j, k)$ with $i \ne j \ne k$ is *proper* when $k = i$ and
of *Tait–Bryan* type when the three are distinct, six sequences of each. With $q_e(\theta) = (\cos
\tfrac\theta2, \sin \tfrac\theta2\, e)$ the rotation by $\theta$ about the coordinate axis $e$, the
rotation by the angles $(\alpha, \beta, \gamma)$ about the three axes, each about the axis as the
previous rotations left it (*intrinsic*), is

$$
	q = q_i(\alpha) \otimes q_j(\beta) \otimes q_k(\gamma), @tag(euler)
$$

smooth in the angles, and the rotation about the fixed axes in the same order (*extrinsic*) is
$q_k(\gamma) \otimes q_j(\beta) \otimes q_i(\alpha)$, the intrinsic rotation of the reversed
sequence. Roll, pitch and yaw $(\phi, \vartheta, \psi)$ in the usual aerospace convention are the
intrinsic $(z, y, x)$ angles $(\psi, \vartheta, \phi)$. The way back has one form for every
sequence.

<Statement {...euler} />

<Proof>

With $c_\theta = \cos \tfrac\theta2$ and $s_\theta = \sin \tfrac\theta2$, and $e_j \times e_i =
-\epsilon\, e_l$, $e_l \times e_i = \epsilon\, e_j$, the product [](eq:product) gives $q_i(\alpha)
\otimes q_j(\beta) = (c_\alpha c_\beta,\ s_\alpha c_\beta\, e_i + c_\alpha s_\beta\, e_j + \epsilon
s_\alpha s_\beta\, e_l)$, and its product with $q_i(\gamma)$ has

$$
	w = c_\beta (c_\alpha c_\gamma - s_\alpha s_\gamma), \quad
	u_i = c_\beta (c_\alpha s_\gamma + s_\alpha c_\gamma), \quad
	u_j = s_\beta (c_\alpha c_\gamma + s_\alpha s_\gamma), \quad
	u_l = \epsilon\, s_\beta (s_\alpha c_\gamma - c_\alpha s_\gamma),
$$

which are the stated forms by the addition formulas. For $\beta \in (0, \pi)$ both $c_\beta$ and
$s_\beta$ are positive, so $\lVert (w, u_i) \rVert = \lambda c_\beta$ and $\lVert (u_j, u_l) \rVert
= \lambda s_\beta$ for $q$ scaled by $\lambda > 0$, and the $\atantwo$s return $\beta / 2$, and
$\sigma$ and $\delta$ modulo $2\pi$; for $-q$ they return $\sigma + \pi$ and $\delta + \pi$ modulo
$2\pi$, which change $\alpha$ and $\gamma$ by multiples of $2\pi$. For the second claim, let $r =
q_j(\tfrac\pi2)$. Since $\pi(r)$ turns $e_i$ about $e_j$ to $e_j \times e_i = -\epsilon e_k$,
$q_k(\gamma) = r \otimes q_i(-\epsilon \gamma) \otimes \bar r$, and so $q \otimes r = q_i(\alpha)
\otimes q_j(\beta + \tfrac\pi2) \otimes q_i(-\epsilon\gamma)$, with $\beta + \tfrac\pi2 \in [0, \pi]$.
Last, $q \otimes (1, e_j) = (w - u_j,\ u + w\, e_j + u \times e_j)$ is $\sqrt 2\, q \otimes r$, and
$u \times e_j = \epsilon u_i\, e_k - \epsilon u_k\, e_i$.

</Proof>

So the Euler angles of a unit quaternion are, for a proper sequence, those of
[%full](statement:euler) read off $q$, and for a Tait–Bryan sequence those read off the linear
rearrangement of $q$ it gives, with $\beta$ lowered by $\tfrac\pi2$ and $\gamma$ multiplied by
$-\epsilon$; for an extrinsic sequence, those of the reversed sequence, in reverse order. They are
the same at $q$ and at $-q$, and so are angles of the rotation. The rotation is smooth in its
angles everywhere. The angles are not smooth in the rotation everywhere, and no choice of formula
makes them so.

<Statement {...eulerSingular} />

<Proof>

For the first claim, let $\tilde\alpha$ be continuous and real-valued, equal to $\alpha$ modulo
$2\pi$ on the loop. Off the lock the angles are unique modulo $2\pi$ by [%full](statement:euler),
so the loop's rotation at $t$ has $\alpha = t$ modulo $2\pi$, and $\tilde\alpha(t) - t$ is
continuous with values in $2\pi\mathbb Z$, hence constant. So $\tilde\alpha$ is larger by $2\pi$ at
$t = 2\pi$ than at $t = 0$, but the loop closes there, since $q_i(2\pi) = -1$ and $\pi(-q) =
\pi(q)$. The same holds of $\gamma$ on its loop. For the second, take a proper sequence. As $\beta
\to 0$, $q_i(\alpha) \otimes q_j(\beta) \otimes q_i(\theta - \alpha)$ tends to $q_i(\theta)$ for
every $\alpha$, and for $\beta \in (0, \pi)$ its angles are $\alpha$ and $\theta - \alpha$ modulo
$2\pi$; so the rotations near $\pi(q_i(\theta))$ take every $\alpha$ and every $\gamma$, while
$\alpha + \gamma = \theta$ on all of them. At $\beta = \pi$ the same holds of $q_i(\alpha) \otimes
q_j(\beta) \otimes q_i(\alpha - \theta)$, which tends to $q_i(\theta) \otimes q_j(\pi)$ for every
$\alpha$, since $q_j(\pi) \otimes q_i(\varphi) = q_i(-\varphi) \otimes q_j(\pi)$, a half-turn about
$e_j$ reversing $e_i$; there $\alpha - \gamma = \theta$. A Tait–Bryan sequence's angles are, by
[%full](statement:euler), the proper angles $(\alpha, \beta + \tfrac\pi2, -\epsilon\gamma)$ of $q
\otimes q_j(\tfrac\pi2)$, a diffeomorphism of the rotations, and its lock is theirs.

</Proof>

The way back therefore has two kinds of singularity.

*The wrap.* Away from the lock, $\alpha$ and $\gamma$ are smooth as points of the circle, and a
formula returns them as real numbers in $(-\pi, \pi]$. By [%full](statement:euler-singular) a
real-valued angle must be cut somewhere, and this convention cuts it where it is $\pi$: crossing
there, it jumps by $2\pi$. The jump is in the value alone. $\atantwo(a, b)$ jumps across its
half-line $a = 0$, $b < 0$, but its derivatives, $(b, -a) / (a^2 + b^2)$, and all of their own, are
continuous across it, so the angles' derivatives of every order are the same on either side of the
wrap. What a jump in value breaks is a difference of values. A difference between an angle $a$ and
an angle $b$ it is compared with is taken on the circle,

$$
	a \ominus b = \atantwo\bigl(\sin(a - b),\ \cos(a - b)\bigr), @tag(angle-residual)
$$

which moves the cut to a difference of $\pm\pi$, as far as an angle can be from the one it is
compared with, and has the derivatives of $a - b$ everywhere else.

*The lock.* At gimbal lock one of the two norms of [%full](statement:euler) vanishes, and only
$\alpha + \gamma$ or $\alpha - \gamma$ is determined: no convention makes the angles continuous
there. Off the lock the angles are smooth, but not uniformly. $\delta$ is the angle of the vector
$(u_j, \epsilon u_l)$ of length $\sin \tfrac\beta2$, and $\sigma$ that of $(w, u_i)$ of length $\cos
\tfrac\beta2$, so the first derivatives of $\alpha$ and $\gamma$ grow like $1 / \sin \tfrac\beta2$
as $\beta \to 0$ and $1 / \cos \tfrac\beta2$ as $\beta \to \pi$, and their second derivatives like
the squares. The same factor amplifies a rounding of $q$'s coordinates into $\alpha$ and $\gamma$
near the lock, while $\alpha \pm \gamma$, read off the larger norm, stays accurate. Euler angles
are a way into and out of a computation; the uncertainty of a rotation is put in a chart.
